  "SEO_description_draft": "A single global square gives explicit quadratic SOS-density error bounds on every fixed full-dimensional compact convex body with Lebesgue measure.",
  "SEO_title_draft": "Quadratic convergence of SOS-density bounds on convex bodies | Alec Kriebel",
  "abstract": {
    "label": "Original abstract",
    "complete_TeX_substring": "ORIGINAL_ABSTRACT.tex",
    "sha256": "37ed9f2dcba4d7de4c23f43cd46ebf472d54498579f0a09ed1a6cecd29bf29ec",
    "visible_complete_one_paragraph": "For every fixed compact convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ with nonempty interior and every real polynomial $f$, the normalized Lebesgue sum-of-squares density bound of degree at most $2r$ has error $O_{K,f}(r^{-2})$ for every integer $r\\ge1$. We give a self-contained proof and an explicit constant, using one global square. A quadratic Taylor upper estimator reduces the objective to a scalar endpoint problem. Homothetic contraction compares every sublevel shell with the same core mass that normalizes a fixed power of a Dirichlet polynomial; that mass cancels before summation. This proves the quadratic upper rate in the convex-body/Lebesgue regime of the 2018 Oberwolfach question. A classical interval example shows that the exponent is sharp in the worst case. The proxy and the broad localization and relative-mass mechanisms have established precedents; no firstness or new-method claim is made.",
